                <pb facs="0579" n="480" sameAs="frus:frus1936v02/pg480"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">852.00/2526: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Dodd</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-08-11T17:00:00+01:00">August 11, 1936—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 11—1:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>251. Conversation with representatives of the Foreign Office reveal about the
                    same attitudes the press reports as to German-Spanish relations. The one thing
                    all insist upon is absolute neutrality of Germany and the conviction prevails
                    here that Italy will remain neutral. The protests as to treatment of Germans in
                    Spain will not lead to intervention. Ships are sent in the hope of relief.
                    However, there is <pb facs="0580" n="481" xml:id="pg481"/> widespread diplomatic
                    feeling here that no real decisions can be made soon in Spain, there being no
                    real leadership on either side. Neurath himself said as much but he added that
                    all peoples are committing harikari on account of foolish commercial
                    policies.</p>
                <p>We will cable the Department the moment we learn anything that seems
                    conclusive.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dodd</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
